RL Compliance Incidents: preliminary run (27 Oct 2026)

What we did. We ran the RL triage prompt (v0.3) and preliminary assessment prompt (v0.4) over every compliance incident entered 20 Apr to 20 Oct 2026 with potential compliance impact (excluding Rejected). We used gpt-5.4 in our Azure tenancy, with one incident per call. Assessment covered incidents routed to RL plus 10 reference cases. Inputs were limited to the spec’s field allowlist, with compliance-recommendation text and closure fields excluded. All raw responses and run logs are retained. Total model cost was about US$100.

Results.

(a) Population: 5,329 incidents (2 blocked by the Azure content filter).
(b) RL identified: 912 (17%): 460 Clear RL nexus, 452 RL plus another pathway.
(c) Assessable with reasonable confidence: 64 (7% of b). A further 95 were within stated bounds, 430 had insufficient confidence, and 322 were not assessable from JUNO.

What it tells us.

Most RL incidents can’t be assessed preliminarily from the JUNO record alone. The model raised 2,316 unresolved items: about half are facts the raiser could supply, and half are evidence held outside JUNO, such as credit files, override records and settlement status.
Reasonable confidence is concentrated in well-documented mortgage review incidents, matching established patterns (threshold, policy only, full breach, exception).
Triage is over-inclusive for hardship and vulnerability matters. Many “RL plus another pathway” incidents involve no lending decision.

Limitations.

Results have not yet been validated against completed Compliance Assessments, so (c) reflects the model’s confidence, not proven accuracy. The CA-exists split is also pending.
We used the current record, including later updates, not the record as it stood at CA creation.
Outputs from the reasoning model may vary slightly on re-run.
12 assessments carry internal consistency flags, reported rather than corrected.

Next steps.

Join the CA dataset to measure triage recall, cluster agreement and confidence calibration.
Review whether “substantial hardship” should route to RL on its own.
Consider making the most commonly missing facts mandatory in incident capture for RL-type incidents.


"""One-row-per-incident combined view of population, triage, assessment and unresolved items."""
import pandas as pd
from rl_config import ROOT

O = ROOT / "04_outputs"
rd = lambda f: pd.read_excel(O / f, dtype=str, keep_default_na=False)

a, b, c, u = rd("a_population.xlsx"), rd("b_rl_identification.xlsx"), rd("c_preliminary_ca.xlsx"), rd("c_unresolved.xlsx")

u["item"] = "[" + u["Resolver"] + " | " + u["Type"] + "] " + u["Question or item"]
uu = u.groupby("INC_ID").agg(**{"Unresolved count": ("item", "size"),
                                "Unresolved items": ("item", "\n".join))}.reset_index()

b = b.rename(columns={"Route": "Triage route", "Reason": "Triage reason"})
c = c.drop(columns=["Route", "CA exists"])

df = (a.merge(b, on="INC_ID", how="left")
       .merge(c, on="INC_ID", how="left")
       .merge(uu, on="INC_ID", how="left"))
df["Assessed"] = df["Q2 confidence"].ne("") & df["Q2 confidence"].notna()
df.to_excel(O / "combined_results.xlsx", index=False, freeze_panes=(1, 1))
print(f"Wrote combined_results.xlsx: {len(df):,} rows, {int(df['Assessed'].sum()):,} assessed")
